# Test: a directive VALUE must be an IDENTIFIER — `directive trace=1` is refused.
#
# The normative grammar (`docs/language-reference.md`, "Directives") is
#
#     directive = "directive" IDENTIFIER { "-" IDENTIFIER } [ "=" IDENTIFIER ]
#
# so the value slot admits an identifier and nothing else. Rust gg refuses this
# at PARSE time (`expect_identifier()?` in `src/parser/mod.rs::parse_directive`);
# the self-host now refuses it at parse time too.
#
# ⚠ THIS CELL CANNOT BE CAUGHT BY THE SEMANTIC VALIDATOR, and that is the whole
# reason it has its own fixture. `token_ident` returns "" for a non-identifier
# token, so the self-host parser used to build `IDirective("trace", "")` for
# BOTH `directive trace` (which must be ACCEPTED) and `directive trace=1` (which
# must be REFUSED) — byte-identical AST. A validator reading `IDirective(name,
# value)` is structurally incapable of telling them apart; only the parser can.
#
# Pinned on BOTH lanes: `directive_value_int_error` (Rust gg) and
# `sh_rejects_directive_value_int` (self-host driver). The self-host half is the
# load-bearing one — `self_host_runtime_diff` returns RustRejected BEFORE the
# self-host is invoked (todo/t0870), so a self-host over-acceptance of a
# Rust-rejected program is invisible to the parity gate and has to be asserted
# directly.

directive trace=1

void main():
    print("REACHED — the directive value was NOT validated on this lane")
